### Caveats

- The objective is an analytic proxy (deadline-weighted EBO). Interval merging, finite shop capacity and hub-station coupling mean the proxy and the simulation can disagree; the calibration row shows by how much.
- The AOG penalty per hour is a planning scenario, not a measured cost. The recommended point did not move at ×0.5 or ×2 on the selection seeds, but its net value did: {{RESULT:frontier.sensitivity.penalty_half_vs_double}}.
- The recommendation is insurance against a harsher reliability world: {{RESULT:frontier.low_stress.summary}}
- Every policy depends on the AOG-desk emergency purchase: {{RESULT:frontier.aog_desk.summary}}.
- Selection-to-holdout drift: the recommended point scored {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.selection_aog_hours}} on selection seeds and {{RESULT:frontier.recommended.aog_hours}} on the holdout ({{RESULT:frontier.recommended.selection_shift_pct}}); P2 went from {{RESULT:policies.P2.selection_aog_hours}} to {{RESULT:policies.P2.aog_hours}} ({{RESULT:policies.P2.selection_shift_pct}}). The selection-seed figure for the chosen plan was optimistic, but its paired improvement over P2 replicated ({{RESULT:frontier.recommended_vs_P2.selection_delta}} on selection, {{RESULT:frontier.recommended_vs_P2.delta_aog_hours}} {{RESULT:frontier.recommended_vs_P2.delta_aog_hours_ci}} on holdout).
- Deadline classes are hypothetical, not MEL categories.
- Results hold under the simulated world's assumptions. They show the method and the size of effect it can produce, not what a real airline would gain.

